{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 149,
    "dDurationMs": 4041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The earth completes a full rotation once per\nday, and hence the moon passes overhead pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4190,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much once daily."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5800,
    "dDurationMs": 2709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So then why are there two high and low tides\nevery day?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8509,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Simple: the moon's gravitational attraction\nis stronger the closer you are to it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13379,
    "dDurationMs": 3041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the close side of the earth gets pulled\naway from the middle, which in turn gets pulled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16420,
    "dDurationMs": 1589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away from the far side."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18009,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the earth rotates, these so-called \"tidal\nforces\" cause water on the surface to slosh"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22140,
    "dDurationMs": 5209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around as \"high tides\" twice per day - when\nthe moon is directly over your head and directly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27349,
    "dDurationMs": 1621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below your feet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28970,
    "dDurationMs": 2069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why is gravity weaker at a greater distance?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31039,
    "dDurationMs": 3391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, imagine you're spraying a stream of\nwater at a sheep [flamingo]."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34430,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the sheep's more distant, less of the water\nhits it than when it's close, so there's less"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38570,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force pushing it away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40480,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravity behaves the same way, except it's\nattractive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43480,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are tidal forces from the sun as\nwell: they're weaker than the lunar ones,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47220,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but when the sun and moon line up [on the\nsame or opposite sides of the earth] the tides"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49220,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pack an extra punch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51130,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not just water that sloshes, though - tidal\nforces cause the earth's crust to bulge as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55260,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well - we just don't notice because we get\nlifted along with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58830,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And tidal forces from the earth cause the\nmoon to bulge, too!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62190,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All this bulging and sloshing dissipates some\nof the earth's rotational energy, causing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66010,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it to gradually slow its rotation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68010,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually, the earth will spin slowly enough\nthat the same side of the earth will always"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71170,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "face the moon - just as the same side of the\nmoon already always faces the earth!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75860,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means that in the distant future, a day\nand a lunar month will each have the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79850,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "length: about 50 of our current earth days."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82470,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What's more, if the moon were to get too close\nto us, tidal forces from the earth would be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86530,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stronger than the gravity holding the moon\ntogether, so rocks on the moon's surface would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90610,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "literally start flying off into space, and\nthe moon would disintegrate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94310,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This would also happen if the earth were to\nget too close to the sun - and if you decided"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98340,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fly into a black hole, it's what would\nactually do you in: your spaceship would get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102390,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ripped apart from tip to tail by tidal forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105360,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that's bad tidings."
    } ]
  } ]
}
